Command Line Reference
**********************

.[perex]
All nine DressCode commands with every option on one page, plus the output formats for the terminal, CI and other tools, exit codes, the cache and parallel runs. For a guide rather than a list, follow the link in each command's section.


Commands
========

```shell
dresscode check [paths...]
dresscode fix [paths...]
dresscode baseline [paths...]
dresscode upgrade estimate|plan|run|tasks|status|abort|today [target [version]]
dresscode config
dresscode explain [decision]
dresscode catalogue
dresscode init
dresscode migrate config [file]
dresscode migrate suppressions [paths...]
```

Each command has its own section below, with all its options; the options shared by all commands are in [Options |#Options of Every Command].

Paths are files or directories, relative to the current directory. They take precedence over the `paths` key of the configuration, with one exception: a directory that contains some of the paths from `paths` is narrowed to just those. This way, `dresscode fix .` in the root of the project does not touch anything outside `paths`, and both `check` and `fix` say so right at the start of the output with the words `narrowed to the configured paths`. A directory or file outside `paths` is used as given. When you give no paths and the configuration lists none, the command ends with the error `No paths given and none configured.` rather than guess what to check.

`dresscode --help` prints the commands and the common options, `dresscode <command> --help` the options of one command, and `dresscode --version` the version.


Options of Every Command
========================

These options apply to every command:

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `-c`, `--config <file>` | a configuration file instead of the nearest `dresscode.neon` or `dresscode.php` (or their templates with the `.dist` extension) |
| `--use <name>` | adds a preset or a plugin on top of the configuration; works even without a configuration file; for `init`, the standard and the sets to write; may be given several times, see [below |#A Preset for One Run: --use] |
| `--set <path=value>` | gives a decision a value for one run; the value is written in NEON; may be given several times, see [Configuration |configuration#One Decision for One Run: --set and --only] |
| `--severity <name=severity>` | says how the violations of a decision, section, preset or rule are counted, `name=warning` or `name=error`, on top of the [`severity` |configuration#risky-fixes] key; may be given several times |
| `--no-color` | output without colors; the environment variable `NO_COLOR` does the same, and `FORCE_COLOR` forces colors instead |
| `--version`, `--help` | version, help |

With `--use`, `--only` and `--fix-risky`, a built-in preset is written without the vendor: `--use nette` is the same as `--use dresscode/nette`. So is a package of the vendor `dresscode`, `--only upgrade-nette`; a preset from a plugin of another vendor is written in full (see [Standards and Sets |configuration#Standards and Sets]). Decisions and sections are written by their path, `--only blankLines` or `--only blankLines.aroundMethods`, and on a typo, the message offers the closest known name (<code>''Unknown preset `modernizaton`. Did you mean `modernizations`?''</code>).


dresscode check .[#check]
=========================

`check` reports violations and writes nothing. [Getting Started |getting-started#Check Your PHP Code Without Changing It] shows how to read its output, and its exit code tells CI the result (see [Exit Codes |#Exit Codes]).

These options belong to `check` and `fix`, and where the table says so, also to `baseline`:

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `-f`, `--format <name>` | the output format, see [below |#Output Formats]; also for `baseline` |
| `--diff` | for `check`, shows what `fix` would change; for `fix`, what it changed; only in the `console` format |
| `--only <name>` | narrows the run to the named decision, section, preset, [upgrade package |upgrade#Upgrading a Library You Maintain] or library the data is about, without changing values; also works for `config`, `explain` and `catalogue`, not for `baseline`; may be given several times, see [Configuration |configuration#One Decision for One Run: --set and --only] |
| `--fix-risky`, `--fix-risky=<name>` | applies the [risky fixes |configuration#risky-fixes] of all decisions, or with a name, only of that decision, section, preset, upgrade package or library, not just of those the configuration names in the `fixRisky` key; may be given several times, the name goes after `=`; they are reported either way |
| `--ask-risky` | only for `fix`: fixes as usual, then asks about each remaining risky fix separately, shows its diff and makes those you accept (`y` yes, `n` no, `a` this one and all further ones of that decision, `q` quit); only in an interactive terminal |
| `--max-warnings <n>` | more than `n` [warnings |configuration#risky-fixes] means exit code `1`; without it, warnings do not change the exit code |
| `--stdin <path>` | reads code from standard input as if it were the file at the given path; cannot be combined with paths, see [below |#Code from Standard Input] |
| `--skip-excluded` | skips even a file given by name if the configuration excludes it in `excludePaths`; for hooks and editors that pass file names; also for `baseline` |
| `--no-cache` | processes every file, even one known to be clean; also for `baseline` |
| `--jobs <n>` | the number of worker processes; `1` means running in a single process; also for `baseline` |
| `--strict-rules` | a rule that breaks its contract is an error, not a warning; for developing your own rules; also for `baseline` |


dresscode fix .[#fix]
=====================

`fix` fixes what it can and reports the rest. A file is written only when it has changed and the fixed text parses back to the same syntax tree; a file with a syntax error or one in which a rule failed stays untouched, and so does a file that an editor or another tool saved in the meantime. It takes the options of `check` above; with `--diff`, it shows what it changed, and `--ask-risky` asks about each risky fix that remains.


dresscode baseline .[#baseline]
===============================

`baseline` writes the violations that a fix would leave into the [baseline |suppressions-and-baseline#baseline] file. Run it on code where `fix` has nothing left to fix. Of the options of `check`, it accepts `--format`, `--skip-excluded`, `--no-cache`, `--jobs` and `--strict-rules`.


dresscode init .[#init]
=======================

`init` writes a `dresscode.neon` that fits how the project is written: it chooses the nearest standard and measures where the code departs from it. See [A Configuration Measured from Your Code |generate-configuration].

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `--use <name>` | measures against this standard instead of the nearest one; more `--use` options add sets after it |
| `--set <path=value>` | writes the value whatever the code says |
| `--budget <n>` | how many files the first fix may change, instead of 1% of the files, at least 2 and at most 20 |
| `--yes` | asks nothing and takes the recommended answers |
| `--dry-run` | prints the configuration below the report and writes nothing |
| `--formatter <file>` | reads the configuration of this formatter, even one written in PHP |
| `--jobs <n>` | how many processes share the measuring |
| `-f`, `--format json` | one document for a tool or an AI agent instead of the report |


dresscode upgrade .[#upgrade]
=============================

`upgrade today` rewrites the code to the API of the newer library and PHP versions that the project now requires in `composer.json`. It works even without a configuration, writes in the style the project follows, and offers to install missing upgrade data. It also accepts `--modernize`, `--ask-risky` and `--yes` (see the tables below). `upgrade estimate` estimates, without changing anything, what upgrading to the target versions involves; `upgrade plan` orders it into steps, `upgrade run` carries them out, `upgrade tasks` lists the manual tasks the plan leaves for you, `upgrade status` says where the saved plan stands, and `upgrade abort` restores what an unfinished run changed. See [Upgrade Your Code Step by Step |upgrade].

A bare `dresscode upgrade` lists its commands. These commands measure, plan and carry out the plan, report where it stands, or restore what an unfinished run changed:

| command, option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `upgrade estimate [<target> [<version>]]` | measures what the upgrade would change, without changing anything |
| `upgrade plan [<target> [<version>]]` | prints the estimate and the plan of the upgrade step by step, changing nothing |
| `--save` | with `plan`, also saves the plan into `dresscode-upgrade.neon` |
| `upgrade run [<target> [<version>]]` | with a target, saves the plan and carries it out; without one, carries out the saved plan from wherever the code is now |
| `upgrade tasks` | lists the manual tasks of the saved plan still left in the code, changing nothing |
| `upgrade status` | says where the saved plan stands and what a run keeps so that it can be undone, without running any fix |
| `upgrade abort` | restores the files of an unfinished step, and `composer.json` and the lock file as they were before the versions were raised, then runs `composer install`; with `--dry-run`, it only says what it would restore and what it would leave |
| `--step <n>` | with `run`, carries out step `n` alone |
| `--commit` | with `run`, commits each topic whose step passed the tests |
| `--discard` | after a run interrupted in the middle of a step, lets `run` continue from the code as it is and `plan --save` save a new plan; with `run`, also forgets an uncommitted step the plan has changed since |
| `--test-command <command>`, `--quick-test-command <command>` | the command that tests the project, with `{junit}`; a step after which a test newly fails is reverted |
| `--library`, `--no-library` | plans the project as a library, which widens its constraints, or as an application, without asking |
| `--yes` | with `run`, changes `composer.json` and updates the packages without asking, even outside a terminal; without it and without a terminal, the run does not even call Composer |
| `--types`, `--no-types` | measures with the types from PHPStan, or without them, even where the configuration turns them on, without asking |
| `--offline` | measures without network access, with what it remembers from Packagist and what the lock knows |
| `--paths <path>` | narrows the estimate, and the plan made from it, to files or directories |
| `--format <console,json>` | the estimate, the plan, a run of it, `upgrade tasks` and `upgrade status` as text, or as data for a tool or an AI agent; the document carries the root of the project in `root`, and for a saved plan also `planIdentity`, a fingerprint of the plan file that tells a tool which plan the document is about; `json` never asks |

The remaining command, `upgrade today`, rewrites the code in place:

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `--modernize` | also rewrites the code to newer PHP constructs (the `modernizations` set) |
| `--ask-risky` | after the upgrade, asks about each remaining risky fix separately and shows its diff; only in an interactive terminal |
| `--fix-risky`, `--fix-risky=<name>` | applies the risky fixes of all decisions, or only of the named decision, section, preset, package or library |
| `--only <name>` | narrows the run to an upgrade package, a library, a decision, a section or a preset; may be given several times |
| `--yes` | installs missing data without asking, even outside a terminal |
| `--max-warnings <n>` | ends with exit code 1 when more than `n` warnings are left, for example in CI |

Apart from these, `upgrade today` accepts the options of `fix`: `--paths`, `--config`, `--use`, `--set`, `--format`, `--jobs` and others (see [Options |#Options of Every Command]). It does not accept `--diff`.


dresscode config .[#config]
===========================

`config` prints what the configuration resolves to: the resolved configuration in the shape of the file, with the layer that set each value, and for a decision that does not apply, the reason. See [Configuration |configuration#What Finally Applies: dresscode config].

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `--file <path>` | for `config`: what applies to this one file, including the overrides in `overrides` |
| `--preset <name>` | for `config`: prints the preset itself, with what it builds on, instead of the configuration |
| `-f`, `--format <name>` | `console` for reading or `json` as data |


dresscode explain .[#explain]
=============================

`explain` describes a decision: what it is for, which values it takes and which one it has in this project; with the name of a section, it does the same for all its decisions, and without an argument, for the whole configuration. See [Configuration |configuration#Explaining a Decision: dresscode explain]. With `--format markdown`, it prints the explanation as a document.


dresscode catalogue .[#catalogue]
=================================

`catalogue` lists all decisions the installation knows, including those from plugins and the project's own rules, and marks those the configuration sets with an asterisk. See [Configuration |configuration#All Decisions the Installation Knows: dresscode catalogue]. With `--format json`, it prints the catalogue as data.


dresscode migrate .[#migrate]
=============================

- `migrate config` translates a PHP CS Fixer, PHP_CodeSniffer or Laravel Pint configuration into decisions; without an argument, it takes the one the project has. See [Migrating to DressCode |migration#migrate-config].
- `migrate suppressions` rewrites `phpcs:*` comments to `dresscode:*`; see [the same page |migration#3. Rewrite the Comments].


A Preset for One Run: --use
===========================

`--use` adds a preset or a plugin for one run, the same thing the `use` key otherwise holds. It takes precedence over all layers, including the configuration and the overrides:

```shell
dresscode check --use modernizations        # the configuration plus the modernizations set
dresscode check src --use psr12            # someone else's project without a configuration
```

Without a configuration file, `--use` is all the run has. This way you can try DressCode on someone else's project without writing anything into it (see [Configuration |configuration#Checking Someone Else's Project Without a Configuration]). `--use` does not take decisions; that is what `--set` is for, and the message says so: <code>''Option `--use`: expects a preset or a plugin, `controlFlow.elseAfterReturn=forbidden` given; a decision is set by `--set path=value`.''</code>


Output Formats
==============

The `--format` option chooses the whole shape of the output, not a detail of it; formats cannot be combined.

`console` is the default for the terminal: a header with the configuration, the target PHP version and the scope of the check, then the individual files with their violations (severity, line and column, message, decision path), and finally a summary. The header also names the [baseline |suppressions-and-baseline#baseline] the run uses. A file lists at most 20 violations and summarizes the rest on one line, grouped by decision (`… and 3456 more: 3100 indentation.unit, 340 spacing.call, 16 others`), so that a file written in another style does not flood the terminal; the formats for tools list everything. A violation of a decision that the [`severity` |configuration#risky-fixes] key softened is labeled `warning` instead of `error`. A risky fix the run was not allowed to make is labeled `risky`; when types or `nameResolution` would decide it, the summary counts it as a warning, like the softened violations. For `check`, the summary also says how many violations would be left after `fix`:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Cart.php
  error      1  `declare(strict_types=1)` must be on the line of the opening tag.  file.strictTypes.position
  error      3  Expected 1 blank line before the namespace, 2 found.               blankLines.beforeNamespace
  error      6  Expected 2 blank lines before the class, 1 found.                  blankLines.beforeDeclaration
  risky  14:10  The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.                  mistakes.strictArgument
                Risky because a strict search no longer finds a value of another type.
  error  20:10  A plain string must be written with single quotes.                 literals.quotes
  error  20:47  A plain string must be written with single quotes.                 literals.quotes

1 risky fix waits, the type is unknown: set `typeAnalysis: phpstan`. See https://dresscode.run/types#enable

FOUND  5 violations, 1 warning, a fix leaves none in 1 file
\--

Above the summary, the output counts the risky fixes waiting for permission, grouped by what would decide them, and gives each group advice with a link to the documentation: types (<code>''the type is unknown: set `typeAnalysis: phpstan`''</code>), names of functions in namespaces (`a name may reach a function of the namespace`), or your judgment, where the fix changes behavior by its nature (`changes what the code does`).

When a violation appears in the file only because another violation was fixed in the same run, the `console` format prints it under the one it follows from, as a `Then also` line with its message and path; if it is on a different line, it says which one (`Then also, on line 7:`). The summary then says how many violations follow from others. In the other formats, it is a violation like any other, with a `derivedFrom` field.

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Box.php
  error  6:11  Expected a line break before the opening brace.                braces.position.class
  error  6:11  Expected a line break after the opening brace.                 braces.position.class
  error  6:33  Expected a line break before the opening brace.                braces.position.function
  error  6:33  Expected a line break after the opening brace.                 braces.position.function
  error  6:36  Expected a line break before the method.                       file.statementsPerLine
               Then also: Expected 2 blank lines before the method, 0 found.  blankLines.aroundMethods
  error  6:68  Expected a line break before the opening brace.                braces.position.function
  error  6:68  Expected a line break after the opening brace.                 braces.position.function
  error  6:87  Expected a line break before the closing brace.                braces.position.function
  error  6:89  Expected a line break before the closing brace.                braces.position.class

FOUND  10 violations, 1 of them following from others, a fix leaves none in 1 file
\--

`fix` prints each file it rewrote with the note `rewritten`, and below it whatever the fixed text still violates, with lines and columns in the fixed file. The fixed text cannot show which violations were removed, so the summary counts the violations found and the ones remaining; `--diff` shows exactly what changed:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Cart.php  rewritten
  risky  13:10  The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.  mistakes.strictArgument
                Risky because a strict search no longer finds a value of another type.

1 risky fix waits, the type is unknown: set `typeAnalysis: phpstan`. See https://dresscode.run/types#enable

FIXED  5 violations found, none remaining, 1 warning in 1 file
\--

Colors turn off automatically when the output does not go to a terminal. In a terminal, the decision path of each violation is a link to its description on this website.

`github` is chosen automatically when the run is a step in GitHub Actions, except with `--stdin`, where only an explicit `--format` applies. Each violation is an annotation that appears directly in the diff of the pull request; for `fix`, these are the violations the fix left.

`lines` is for Git hooks and editors: one line per finding in the form compilers use, `path:line:column: severity: message [decision]`; for a risky fix the run did not make, the risk follows the decision. The column is missing where the finding has none, a line break in the message becomes a space, and the path is relative to the working directory, or absolute for a file outside it, and always uses forward slashes. For `fix`, these are the findings the fix left; a clean file and a rewritten file with nothing left print nothing. Warnings about the configuration go to standard error in all formats. The [Editors and IDEs |editors#Findings in the Editor: the lines Format] page shows how an editor reads the output.

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Cart.php:13:10: warning: The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`. [mistakes.strictArgument; risk=typeUnknown]
src/Order.php:16:3: error: The line is 144 characters long, more than 140. [file.longLines]
\--

`agent` is for an AI agent that has just edited the file: sentences instead of a table, for a rewritten file a diff of what is already written, naming the removed imports, and for each remaining violation what to do about it. It leaves out warnings about the configuration. `check`, `fix`, `baseline` and `upgrade today` support it; `init`, `migrate` and the other `upgrade` commands give an agent the `json` format. The [AI Agents |ai-agents] page shows an example and how to set it up.

`checkstyle` is XML that Jenkins, the `cs2pr` tool and other CI tools understand; for `fix`, as with `github`, it contains the violations the fix left.

`json` is for your own tools, see [the next section |#JSON Output for Tools].


JSON Output for Tools
=====================

If you want to integrate DressCode into your own tool, such as a code generator, a migration script or a service that checks code from a form, run it from the command line with `--format json` and read the result. The classes that assemble the run are internal and may change in a minor version; the shape of this document will not (see [DressCode Backward Compatibility |compatibility]).

```shell
dresscode check --format json src/Cart.php
```

```json
{
    "version": 1,
    "mode": "check",
    "root": "/var/www/shop",
    "files": [
        {
            "path": "src/Cart.php",
            "violations": [
                {
                    "decision": "mistakes.strictArgument",
                    "message": "The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.",
                    "line": 19,
                    "column": 10,
                    "severity": "warning",
                    "fixable": true,
                    "risk": "typeUnknown",
                    "refused": true,
                    "because": "a strict search no longer finds a value of another type",
                    "fingerprint": "2f83e4aa829e87db",
                    "derivedFrom": null
                }
            ],
            "remaining": [
                {
                    "decision": "mistakes.strictArgument",
                    "message": "The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.",
                    "line": 19,
                    "column": 10,
                    "severity": "warning",
                    "fixable": true,
                    "risk": "typeUnknown",
                    "refused": true,
                    "because": "a strict search no longer finds a value of another type",
                    "fingerprint": "2f83e4aa829e87db",
                    "derivedFrom": null
                }
            ],
            "warnings": [],
            "syntaxError": null,
            "failure": null,
            "changed": false,
            "written": false
        }
    ],
    "summary": {
        "files": 1,
        "violations": 1,
        "remaining": 1,
        "refused": 1,
        "changedFiles": 0,
        "syntaxErrors": 0,
        "failures": 0,
        "baselined": 0,
        "exitCode": 0
    },
    "warnings": []
}
```

The header gives the version of the format in `version`, whether it was `check` or `fix` (`mode`), and the root of the project in `root`, to which the file paths are relative. The `files` array contains only files with something to report; clean files are not in it. Each file has:

- `violations`: the violations found in the file as it was loaded;
- `remaining`: the violations left after the fix, with lines and columns in the fixed text, that is, exactly what the next check of the fixed file will report;
- `warnings`: warnings about the file, for example about a rule that broke its contract;
- `syntaxError`: the file cannot be parsed, with the message in `message` and the line in `line`, otherwise `null`;
- `failure`: a rule failed in the file or the rules did not agree, with the message in `message` and a link to the documentation in `docs`, otherwise `null`;
- `changed`: the fix changes the text (for `check`, it would change it), and `written`: the file was written.

A violation carries the decision path in `decision`, the message in `message`, the line in `line` and the column in `column`, counted from 1 with a tab as one character (`null` for a violation in whitespace or in a comment, and for a token that only the fix wrote). The severity is in `severity` (`error` or `warning`), and `fixable` says whether a fix exists for the violation (`false` for a finding the rule only reports, and for a decision the project lists in `reportOnly` to have it only reported). For a risky fix, `risk` says what would decide it: `typeUnknown` (the type of a value), `nameUncertain` (the name of a function in a namespace), or `behaviorChanges` (the fix changes behavior by its nature); otherwise it is `null`. `refused` says that the run did not make the risky fix because there was no permission for it, and `because` adds a sentence about what exactly can go wrong at that place when the risk alone does not explain it. `fingerprint` is the fingerprint under which the [baseline |suppressions-and-baseline#baseline] knows the violation, and `derivedFrom` is the fingerprint of the violation whose fix led to this one.

`summary` sums up the run: the number of processed files in `files`, the violations found in `violations`, those remaining in `remaining`, the refused risky fixes in `refused`, the files the fix changes in `changedFiles`, the files with a syntax error in `syntaxErrors`, the files where a rule failed in `failures`, the violations the baseline silenced in `baselined`, and the exit code of the run in `exitCode`. At the end come the `warnings` about the whole run, for example about stale baseline entries.

The keys of the structure are camelCase; the data (messages, paths) stays as the tool or you wrote it. Files are printed as they are processed, so even on a large project, you do not have to wait until the end for the output. Code that is not on disk can be checked or fixed through [standard input |#Code from Standard Input].


Exit Codes
==========

| code | meaning |
|---|---|
| `0` | done, and nothing is left; for `fix` also when the fixed text no longer violates anything; warnings do not change the exit code unless `--max-warnings` sets a threshold |
| `1` | done, but work is left for a person: violations, a file that cannot be parsed, more warnings than `--max-warnings` allows, a `baseline` refused over code that `fix` would still change, manual tasks of an upgrade, or what a migration did not carry over; for a run of the [upgrade plan |upgrade#Carrying Out the Plan] also a step reverted because of the tests, more findings of the check at the end of the plan, or a previous run interrupted in the middle of a step; for `upgrade abort`, a file it left unchanged, or a failed `composer install` |
| `2` | incomplete, because something could not be processed: a rule threw an exception in a file, the rules got into a loop, or the file changed during the fix; an estimate, a plan, a run or the tasks of an upgrade missed a file, an estimate or a plan missed upgrade data or an answer from Composer; `init` measured without the files a rule failed in; the tests fail to run even before the first step of `upgrade run`; Git refused a commit of `upgrade run --commit`; `migrate suppressions` found a name nothing covers, or skipped a file |
| `3` | an error on the command line or in the configuration: an unknown option, key or value, a missing configuration |
| `4` | a run of the upgrade plan waits for a step outside DressCode: for the versions in `composer.json` to be raised, also when Composer did not finish raising them, or at a manual step until the lock file allows the target version; for DressCode running inside another program, also after the versions are raised, after which `upgrade run` has to be run again |

These codes apply to every command. The queries that change no code, `upgrade estimate` and `upgrade plan`, end with `0` even when they find a lot of work, because that work is their answer, and with `2` when they could not examine everything; `upgrade status` always ends with `0`.

The failure of one file does not stop the run: the file is reported as failed, nothing is written into it, and the run continues with the other files. A risky fix that waits for permission and changes the behavior of the code by its nature is a violation like any other, and it keeps the exit code at `1` until someone applies it or adds it to the baseline; one that types or `nameResolution` would decide is a warning.


Cache and Parallel Runs
=======================

DressCode remembers every file it found clean: its path and a fingerprint of its content, and with them a fingerprint of the configuration that applies to it (including the overrides for its path), of the target PHP version, of the baseline and of the versions of the installed packages. The path is part of the key because the same content in two files may get different verdicts, for example because of an override. Next time, a file with the same path, content and configuration is skipped; as soon as any of these things changes, it is processed again. The counts in the summary and the warnings about stale baseline entries are the same with or without the cache. DressCode remembers content written by `fix` only in a project without a baseline. The cache lives in the system temporary directory, or wherever `cacheDir` in the configuration points; `--no-cache` bypasses it for one run.

Files the cache does not cover are split among worker processes. By default, there are as many as there are CPUs, but at most one process per four files, because starting a process has a cost. `--jobs 1` runs without worker processes, which helps when debugging your own rule, and `--jobs 8` helps on a machine with many cores, where the default estimate would be needlessly low. `baseline` uses worker processes too, so generating it takes no longer than a check.


Code from Standard Input
========================

`--stdin` is the interface for hooks, editors and other tools: the code arrives on standard input, and the path says what applies to it (the overrides are matched against this path). `check --stdin` gives a report in any format. `fix --stdin` is a formatter: it prints the whole fixed code to standard output and nothing else, exits with code 0 even when violations remain in the code, and refuses the report options (`--format`, `--diff`, `--max-warnings`, `--ask-risky`); [the page about editors |editors#A Formatter over Standard Input] describes what it does on a syntax error and on a failure.

```shell
git show :src/Cart.php | dresscode check --stdin src/Cart.php -f lines
cat src/Cart.php | dresscode fix --stdin src/Cart.php > fixed.php
```

You cannot combine `--stdin` with paths to other files. The cache is not used for standard input. The `excludePaths` key is not evaluated: the code is checked even when the path lies in an excluded part of the project; with `--skip-excluded`, `fix --stdin` returns the code unchanged for such a path, and `check --stdin` reports nothing.


DressCode Inside Your Own Tool
==============================

If you bundle DressCode into your own tool with its own binary, run the whole command-line application, the `DressCode\Console\Application` class, inside your process, and give it the configuration that applies when the project has none of its own:

```php
use DressCode\Config;
use DressCode\Console\Application;

$application = new Application(script: __FILE__, defaultConfig: new Config(use: ['nette'], paths: ['src', 'tests']));
exit($application->run($argv));
```

`script` is your binary: DressCode uses it to start the parallel worker processes and an upgrade run that continues in a new process, so that they get the same default configuration. Everything else (the commands, options, formats and parallel runs) stays the same as with `dresscode`, and `run()` returns the same exit codes. `run()` itself prints the errors that belong to the run to standard error: an invalid call and a bad configuration with exit code `3`, a rule failure, rules in a loop, and environment errors of the `RuntimeException` type, for example a file that cannot be written or a crashed worker process, with exit code `2`. Other exceptions, for example `LogicException` or `Error`, indicate a bug in the program, and `run()` lets them propagate.

{{description: The DressCode command line reference: check, fix, baseline, init, upgrade, config, explain, catalogue and migrate with all their options, output formats for CI and editors, exit codes, cache and parallel runs.}}
